            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 48-year-old female, admitted on 30/04/2010 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point with rebound tenderness. Blood tests demonstrated leukocytosis with left shift. Abdominal ultrasonography confirmed an inflamed, non-perforated appendix. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy on 01/05/2010 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, intravenous fluid replacement and analgesic medication were administered. Wound care was performed daily. Vital signs remained stable throughout the stay. The patient recovered well and was discharged home on 04/05/2010 in good general condition. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks for wound inspection and pathology result review. Do not hesitate to contact me with any questions.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
